---
import Study from "../../layouts/Study.astro";
import Pipeline from "../../components/Pipeline.astro";
---

<Study
  title="WDCGG Data Pipeline"
  kind="Research"
  status="Research software / Peer-review revision"
  repo="wdcgg-data-pipeline"
  deck="What has to happen before a long-term atmospheric record can support an environmental conclusion?"
>
  <Pipeline />
  <div class="study-grid">
    <aside>
      <p>Focus</p>
      <span>
        Data provenance
        <br />
        Quality control
        <br />
        Reproducibility
      </span>
    </aside>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Start with the source.</h2>
      <p>
        This Python research pipeline screens, harmonises and analyses WDCGG CO₂
        and CH₄ observations. It ties each archive to a frozen manifest using
        SHA-256, aligns UTC timestamps and units, and retains contributor-valid
        observations before calculating monthly means.
      </p>
      <p>
        The study screened 362 station–gas catalogue products and acquired 38
        official datasets spanning 23 physical stations. The primary Core cohort
        comprises 26 station–gas products at 14 stations. A product is a
        station–gas series, not an independent station.
      </p>
      <h2>What the result supports.</h2>
      <p>
        The frozen Core summaries report same-month Seasonal Sen trends of{" "}
        <strong>2.5113 ppm yr⁻¹ for CO₂</strong> and{" "}
        <strong>11.3146 ppb yr⁻¹ for CH₄</strong>. These describe a selected,
        non-random cohort. They are not global atmospheric averages.
      </p>
      <p>
        Primary environmental findings use observed-only records. Statistical
        anomaly flags are review annotations, not automatic deletion.
        Gap-reconstruction benchmarks do not justify filling every missing
        observation.
      </p>
      <a
        class="text-link"
        href="https://github.com/KoreyoshiE/wdcgg-data-pipeline/blob/main/results/frozen/headline_results.json"
      >
        Inspect frozen headline results ↗
      </a>
      <details>
        <summary>Methods and reproducibility</summary>
        <div>
          <p>
            Core qualification uses 75% within-month and 75% station-period
            coverage; Extended uses 75% / 60%. A 20-cell sensitivity grid
            recomputes qualification independently.
          </p>
          <p>
            Validation includes physical-station-held-out anomaly detection and
            repeated gap reconstruction. Trend uncertainty uses a run-aware
            residual moving-block bootstrap, with a 12-month primary block and
            6/24-month sensitivity checks.
          </p>
          <p>
            Offline tests run on synthetic fixtures. A full rerun requires the
            38 source archives under provider terms, substantial compute, and
            the documented source layout.
          </p>
          <a href="https://github.com/KoreyoshiE/wdcgg-data-pipeline/blob/main/docs/REPRODUCIBILITY.md">
            Reproduction guide ↗
          </a>
        </div>
      </details>
      <div class="limitation">
        <h3>Status and limits</h3>
        <p>
          The associated DSIT 2026 manuscript remains under peer-review
          revision. No acceptance, DOI or proceedings publication is claimed.
          Raw third-party observations are not redistributed. This portfolio
          describes the public software and frozen outputs; it does not claim an
          independent full-study rerun.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2>Software as part of the evidence.</h2>
      <p>
        The public repository contains executable modules, configuration,
        offline tests, source manifests and compact frozen results. Its
        contribution is a traceable path between acquired records,
        methodological decisions and final summaries.
      </p>
      <a class="next-study" href="/research/shanghai-ozone/">
        <small>Continue reading</small>
        <span>Shanghai Ozone ML&nbsp;↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</Study>
